{
  "id": "proj-doorway-pull-up-bar-rig",
  "slug": "doorway-pull-up-bar-rig",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Doorway Pull-Up Bar Rig",
  "summary": "A wall-mounted bar on steel brackets into the studs. Stronger than any door-frame bar.",
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedHours": 2,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Find the framing above the door with the stud finder and confirm it with a finish nail in a spot the trim will hide. There is a solid header over the opening with a stud on each side; plan a ledger that crosses at least two studs, with three lag screws in each.",
    "Check the headroom: with the bar just above the door trim (about 84 in), an 8 ft ceiling leaves about 12 in, which is the least you want for your head to clear the bar.",
    "Cut the 2x6 ledger long enough to cross those studs (about 40 in for most doorways) and sand the edges.",
    "Assemble the U on the floor: thread an elbow onto each end of the bar and a nipple into each elbow, then tighten with the pipe wrench until both arms are parallel and the whole U lies flat.",
    "Spin a floor flange tight onto the end of each arm. The flanges turn freely because nothing is mounted yet, so you can tighten them without knocking the U out of line.",
    "Centre the flanges on the ledger, mark their holes, and drill 1/4 in through. Counterbore the back of the ledger with the Forstner bit so the carriage-bolt heads sit flush against the wall, then bolt through with lock nuts.",
    "With a helper holding the ledger level on the wall, drill 3/16 in pilot holes into each stud and drive the lag screws with washers until snug. Stop there: overdriving strips the wood.",
    "Degrease the pipe and wrap the grip in hockey tape. Test it with a dead hang, then a gentle bounce, and check the lag screws again after the first week. Do not kip on it: a swinging body puts several times its weight on the screws."
  ],
